WEBVTT

00:00:27.246 --> 00:00:30.371
Aristotle focused on oration though,

00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:33.454
and he described three types
of persuasive speech.

00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:36.121
Forensic, or judicial rhetoric

00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:38.746
establishes facts and judgements
about the past,

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:40.954
similar to detectives at a crime scene.

00:00:41.621 --> 00:00:44.537
Epideictic, or demonstrative rhetoric

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:47.079
makes a proclamation
about the present situation,

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:48.579
as in wedding speeches.

00:00:48.621 --> 00:00:51.704
But we way to accomplish change
is through deliberative rhetoric

00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:53.412
or “symbouleutikon”.

00:00:53.454 --> 00:00:55.204
Rather than the past or the present,

00:00:55.246 --> 00:00:58.621
deliberative rhetoric
focuses on the future.

00:00:58.662 --> 00:01:00.371
It’s the rhetoric of politicians